In article <46633l$psf@news.rrz.uni-koeln.de>,
   rw@MI.Uni-Koeln.DE (Ralf Wirowski) writes:
|>We want to connect out DECstation 3000/600 under DEC-Unix 3.2c to a VAX 
|>running VMS via an asynchronous Modem and DECnet Phase IV. 
|>We bought DECnet/OSI for DEC-Unix 3.2c which handle the Phase IV-Connection 
|>too. But there is NO direct support for an asyn. Modem like in VMS/DEcnet.

Hi Ralf,

You are correct that our product does not support asynch lines; it is
functionality that even our old Phase IV product (DECnet-ULTRIX) never
had.  We do support synchronous circuits using HDLC, which requires
the Wide Area Device Drivers product.

If you truly require an asynch line, we do support running DECnet
over TCP, so you could conceivably use SLIP.  Of course, then the
VMS system on the other end of the line would have to be running
DECnet/OSI as well, to get the DECnet over TCP/IP support.  So even
this solution would not be cost free.

|>In the setup-tools for DECnet/OSI are possibilities to define an asyn. modem
|>as the lowest layer, but we need an idea to get the upper layers correct.

Yes, we support the Modem Connect entity, but it's there to support
synchronous lines for HDLC (or LAPB, if X.25 is configured).  Or have
I misunderstood you?

Dan Harrington
DECnet/OSI Engineering



