Jean-Louis Moser (jlm@ludo.two-oo-one.fr) wrote:

: | May be here is a bug in DEC C++ V5.0-3 for DEC OSF/1 (Alpha).

: #include <iostream.h>
: #include <stdarg.h>

: void foo (float a, ...)
: {
:     va_list list;
:     va_start (list, a);
:     while (a != 0) {
: 	cout << a << " ";
: 	a = va_arg (list, float);
:     }
:     cout << endl; va_end (list);
: }

: void main ()
: {
:     foo (1.0f,2.0f,3.0f,4.0f,0);
: }

: the result is : 1 (it may be: 1 2 3 4)

: when we replace float with short, int, double, or struct A {long x_; long y_;};
: it work fine. There is the same problem with cc and GNU g++.

From Programmer's Quick Reference Series, Standard C (Plauger & Brodie), Page
142 (va_start)
The last argument must not have register storage class, and it must have a 
type  that is not changed by the translator. (It cannot have an array type,
a function type, type float, or any integer type that changes when promoted).

Your va_start does not meet these requirements.
