Hello Net OSFers,

  I have a brand new Alcor running DUX (Digital Unix) 3.2;
it has the new PC style keyboard (meaning where-normally-would-be-the
PF1-key is now replaced by the "Num Lock" key).

  We have a requirement to log on to the VMS boxes.
It's essential that the keypad would map properly in EDT/EVE, etc.

  Digital gave us a xmodmap file which we can run to remap.
But since I need to do this globally at system level for every
user that would log on to this box, I'd *prefer* a different way.

  On other platforms, we always do it in /usr/lib/X11/app-defaults/XTerm
as follows:

XTerm*VT100.Translations:     #override Meta<KeyPress>: insert-seven-bit()

VMSTERM*VT100.Translations: #override <Key>KP_Multiply: string(0x1b) string(OP)\
n\
 <Key>KP_Divide: string(0x1b) string(OQ)\n\
 <Key>KP_Add: string(0x1b) string(OR)\n\
 <Key>KP_Subtract: string(0x1b) string(OS)\n\
 <Key>KP_Enter: string(0x1b) string(Om)\n\
 <Key>KP_Tab: string(0x1b) string(OM)\n\
 <Key>BackSpace: string(0x7f)
 <Key>KP_Tab: string(0x1b) string(OM)\n\
 <Key>BackSpace: string(0x7f)


I want to do the same for OSF.   This way, the keypad doesn't get remapped
until a xterm is explicitly invoked as VMSTERM.

I am curious to know:
   1)  have anyone done this already?
   2)  how do you remap the keypad for VMS?
   3)  If already done so, would someone email be a copy of
       the XTerm redefinition?  

thanks for any info!

please email to bao@ug.eds.com as usually do not monitor this group.

